  "summary": "Astonished-looking carved ancestor figures of Geelvink Bay, seating departed souls in their bodies.",
  "description": "Korwar figures carve a seated ancestor with hands on knees, head tilted up, and startlingly empty or shell-inlaid eyes — vessels where the recently dead's spirit could be asked to stay benevolent rather than linger as danger. Carvers worked skulls of honored kin into some figures; others held protective powers for voyagers. Geelvink (Cenderawasih) Bay villages produced them until Dutch missionization suppressed the rites, and surviving figures — some made expressly for sale to collectors — became icons of Oceanic art.",
